- Retrieved practices:
- CWE-120: Buffer Copy without Checking Size of Input
WHAT: Failing to allocate enough storage for strings (including the null terminator) or copying strings without checking destination size can cause buffer overflows and off-by-one writes/reads.
WHY: Buffer overflows and out-of-bounds accesses can corrupt memory, leak data, or enable arbitrary code execution (CWE-119, CWE-123, CWE-125, CWE-193).
HOW: Always allocate string buffers with space for all characters plus the null terminator; use size-aware APIs that limit copies to buffer length; validate and constrain tainted input lengths before formatted I/O or string-copy operations.
EXAMPLE (C): // Before: char buf[10]; strcpy(buf, user_input);
// After:  char buf[10]; strncpy(buf, user_input, sizeof(buf)-1); buf[sizeof(buf)-1] = '\0';

- Guarantee sufficient storage for strings and prevent sprintf() buffer overflows
WHAT: Failing to allocate enough space for strings (including the null terminator) or using sprintf() without bounding the output can cause buffer overflows when handling external or user-controlled strings.
WHY: Buffer overflows can corrupt memory, crash the program, or enable arbitrary code execution.
HOW: Always allocate string buffers with space for all characters plus the null terminator, and when formatting strings, use bounded operations or precision specifiers so the total written bytes never exceed the buffer size.
EXAMPLE: Noncompliant (C): `char filename[128]; sprintf(filename, "%s.txt", name);`
Compliant (C): `char filename[128]; sprintf(filename, "%.123s.txt", name);`

- Detect and handle standard library allocation errors
WHAT: Failing to check and correctly handle errors from memory-allocation functions (calloc(), realloc()) can lead to null dereferences, memory leaks, or undefined behavior.
WHY: Unhandled allocation failures can cause crashes, data corruption, or resource exhaustion, impacting availability and integrity.
HOW: Always check the return value of calloc()/realloc() for NULL before use; on failure, avoid dereferencing the pointer, preserve the original pointer when using realloc() (assign to a temporary), and perform any necessary cleanup or error reporting.
EXAMPLE:
Before (noncompliant, realloc): `p = realloc(p, new_size); if (p == NULL) { /* Handle error, but original block lost */ }`
After (compliant): `void *tmp = realloc(p, new_size); if (tmp == NULL) { /* Handle error, p still valid */ } else { p = tmp; }`

- Detect and handle standard library allocation failures
WHAT: Failing to check the return value of calloc() (or other allocation functions) and then using the resulting pointer (e.g., pointer arithmetic, memcpy) can lead to writes to attacker-controlled addresses when the pointer is NULL.
WHY: An attacker can force allocation failure with large, tainted sizes and then choose values (such as indices or offsets) that cause memory corruption and potentially arbitrary code execution.
HOW: Treat all allocation sizes derived from untrusted input as tainted; validate them against strict bounds, call calloc()/malloc(), and immediately check for NULL before any pointer arithmetic or dereference; on failure, abort the operation and avoid using the pointer.
EXAMPLE (C):
Before: `signal_info *start = calloc(num_of_records, sizeof(signal_info)); signal_info *point = start + temp_num - 1; memcpy(point->sig_desc, tmp2, SIG_DESC_SIZE);`
After: `signal_info *start = calloc(num_of_records, sizeof(signal_info)); if (start == NULL) { /* handle error and return */ } signal_info *point = start + temp_num - 1; memcpy(point->sig_desc, tmp2, SIG_DESC_SIZE);`



